Where does git fetch actually store the data?


When you run:
         git fetch origin
Git does three key things behind the scenes:
1. Downloads commits, branches, and tags from the remote
     * Git connects to the remote (e.g., GitHub)
     * It fetches the latest objects (commits, trees, blobs)
     * But it does NOT touch your local branches

2. Stores them as remote-tracking branches
Fetched data is stored in:
             .git/refs/remotes/origin/<branch-name>
For example:
origin/main
origin/dev
origin/feature/login

These are not real branches you can directly work on — they're pointers to the latest state of the remote branches.
They help you compare your local branches with the remote.


3. Commits are saved in Git’s object database
Git stores the actual commit data inside:
    .git/objects/
This is where all fetched commits (and associated data) live — compressed and hashed.
Git just adds them to your local database, without merging anything.


Example:
You run:
        git fetch origin
Then check:
        git log main..origin/main
This shows commits that were fetched into origin/main —
 but are not yet merged into your local main.





git fetch is like downloading a new episode — but not playing it until you decide (via merge, rebase, etc.)
You get full visibility, but zero disruption to your code.
